  "worked_solution": "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\u27e8z,r\u27e9=\u27e85\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9,\\,\u03d5\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,\u03d5) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8(5\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e85\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $\u03d5\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta .$",
